 ## Background
-The concept of early ray termination is fundamental to efficient volume rendering, particularly in medical imaging and scientific visualization where datasets are large and often sparse. The mathematical foundation of front-to-back compositing allows for the accumulation of opacity, which serves as the basis for deterministic termination [S2, unofficial]. The integration of probabilistic methods like Russian Roulette into volume rendering adapted techniques from path tracing to ensure unbiased results when terminating low-contribution paths [1371-russian-roulette.md]. Recent developments, such as Recursive-NeRF, have extended these concepts to neural radiance fields, using uncertainty prediction to dynamically grow networks and terminate ray marching early based on learned confidence metrics [S1].
+The concept of early ray termination is fundamental to image-order volume rendering, particularly in the context of brick-based rendering and GPU optimization. Danny Ruijters' work on optimizing GPU volume rendering highlights the use of early ray termination to reduce load on rasterization and fragment shader bottlenecks by leveraging depth buffers for saturated rays [S2, unofficial]. More recently, uncertainty-based early termination has been integrated into neural rendering frameworks like Recursive-NeRF to dynamically prune computation based on predicted uncertainty [S1].
 
